# shir_nodes/walk_dir.node — STREAMING directory-tree iteration (find).
#
# The directory-source primitive: reduces `find PATH -type f|-type d
# [-maxdepth N]` and `find … | wc -l` pipelines to a lazy per-entry loop —
# the tree is walked entry-by-entry (O(depth) memory beyond the loop var),
# never materialized as a list, and file CONTENTS are never opened.
# Body statements use the existing VALUE primitives per entry. See
# docs/shir-primitives.md §WalkDir for the pinned GNU find semantics.

node WalkDir
tag "WalkDir"
field source: expr
field var: string
field body: stmts
field type_filter: optional_string
# -name GLOB (basename glob match); renderers implement fnmatch-style
field name_filter: optional_string
field maxdepth: optional_expr
# Unstated — the missing component is a DIRECTORY TREE with per-entry identity: a
# name→child map, entry metadata, order freedom for the traversal, and a symlink/cycle
# policy. The intended law (the visited multiset equals the subtree's entries exactly
# once, every path root-prefixed, none escaping the root) requires that tree model plus a
# well-foundedness argument for cycles, neither of which exists in this theory.
# Recorded as a named missing model rather than the bare word "effect".
# Spec: TextOps#walk_dir_sound (docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Strings.thy, section
# "WalkDir: the visited path set"). Model: a finite tree of name→child pairs, paths as
# name lists from the root, an entry set defined by a set-level recursion, and a fold that
# emits each child's own path and its descendants. Proved for any tree: the visited SET is
# exactly the entry set; no empty path is visited (nothing at or above the root); every
# entry declines into a real child (so none escapes the root); the emission COUNT equals
# the entry count, which together with the set law pins "exactly once each" (a duplicate
# would cost a dropped entry, which the set law forbids); and running the fold with the
# child list REVERSED leaves the visited set unchanged, so order is free.
#
# OPEN REFINEMENT: the injective list form `distinct (walk t)` needs the per-child
# disjointness lemma (paths from distinct child names cannot coincide) under the `wf_tree`
# hypothesis that child names are distinct.
#
# NOT PROMISED: the traversal order itself and the order of opendir results; SYMLINKS AND
# CYCLES (a finite tree cannot represent them, so a real walker needs a visited-inode set
# and a cycle policy); entry metadata, permissions, name encoding; IO errors; and laziness
# (this fold materialises the list).
spec: TextOps#walk_dir_sound
